#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:0
msgid ""
"Turn a USB stick into Ubuntu installation media from Ubuntu, Windows or "
"macOS so you can try or install Ubuntu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:7
msgid "Topics: Installation Windows macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:11
msgid "Create a bootable USB stick"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:13
msgid ""
"You can convert your USB stick (USB flash drive) into Ubuntu installation "
"media. This is different from copying a file to the USB stick. Instead, use "
"a specialized application to rewrite your USB stick with Ubuntu. Select an "
"application depending on the system that you're currently using."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:15
msgid "With a bootable Ubuntu USB stick, you can:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:17
msgid "Install or upgrade Ubuntu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:18
msgid ""
"Test out the Ubuntu Desktop experience without touching your PC "
"configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:19
msgid "Boot into Ubuntu on a borrowed machine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:20
msgid "Use tools from the USB stick to repair or fix a broken configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:22
msgid "You need:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:24
msgid "An 8GB USB stick or larger."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:27
msgid ""
"Back up your files on the USB stick. This process will overwrite and destroy "
"all data stored there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:30
msgid ""
"An Ubuntu image file. See [Download Ubuntu Desktop](https://ubuntu.com/"
"download/desktop)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:32
msgid ""
"Take note of where your browser saves the downloaded file. This is normally "
"a directory called *Downloads*. Don't download the ISO image directly to the "
"USB stick."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:34
msgid "Proceed only when the download has successfully finished."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:37
msgid "On Ubuntu and other Linux distributions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:39
msgid ""
"Ubuntu Desktop comes with the Disks application by default, which you can "
"use to write the image. Other Linux distributions provide a wide range of "
"different image writer applications. Choose the first one that's available "
"on your system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:41
msgid "Using Disks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:43
msgid ""
"The Disks application is installed by default on Ubuntu Desktop and on most "
"Linux distributions that use the GNOME desktop environment. We recommend "
"choosing it if it's available on your system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:45
msgid ""
"Disks can also write images of any other operating system, including Linux "
"distributions or Microsoft Windows."
msgstr ""

