#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:107
msgid "Using KDE ISO Image Writer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:109
msgid ""
"KDE ISO Image Writer is part of the KDE Plasma desktop environment. You can "
"use it on distributions like Kubuntu. It's available for Ubuntu and Kubuntu "
"26.04 LTS and later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:111
msgid ""
"Go to the [releases.ubuntu.com](https://releases.ubuntu.com/) website and "
"find the Ubuntu release that you're writing on your USB stick, such as "
"[Ubuntu 26.04 LTS (Resolute Raccoon)](https://releases.ubuntu.com/resolute/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:113
msgid ""
"Download the `SHA256SUMS` and `SHA256SUMS.gpg` checksum files. In the file "
"selector, make sure not to attach the `.txt` suffix to the `SHA256SUMS` "
"file. Place the files in the same directory as the Ubuntu image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:115
msgid "KDE ISO Image Writer uses these files to verify the Ubuntu image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/create-a-bootable-usb-stick.md:117
#: ../../../reuse/usb-using-balena-etcher-on-macos.txt:34
